extremists have long known that the  Internet is a place where mindsets and  behaviors can be shaped but to challenge  the sermons of hatred and the propaganda  videos calling for jihad a new character  has stepped into this domain he is a  cartoon called Abdullah X and his  mission is to win young minds by  directly challenging radical beliefs you  have to kill others to make your world  pure this is what you think his armies  are you for real  his creator has never been interviewed  before and doesn't want to be identified  I have the knowledge the background and  the experience to understand that  narratives such as the one driven by  extremist groups have beaten mainstream  society in in access to the Internet  they have beaten mainstream society in  content in the ability to justify what  they stand for and what they believe in  and I felt - in 2012 for example when I  started this project that unless we make  a proactive step to take some of that  ground back online it will never embed  itself in the offline reality of young  people the man behind Abdullah X knows  this because he himself was a former  hardline extremist who followed the  sermons of preachers such as Abu Hamza  and Omar Bakri and was a member of  banned extremist groups his character is  also a disillusioned Muslim but on his  cartoon journey comes to realize violent  jihad is not the answer  how did you come to that conclusion  because you you obviously used to think  the other way  I came to that conclusion probably  through a mixture of two realizations  one the people spewing out this message  90% of them didn't have the heart or  conviction to go and do anything like  this themselves you mean how hamza  backery those kind of people certainly  individual certainly individuals that  I've come into contact with who adopted  the mantra of being a sheikh or a  scholar ultimately became the same  individuals who were happy to claim  government benefits I realized therefore  that unless I'm confident that I have  the appropriate picture of my faith I  will always be seeing it through the  lens of these kind of people that was  that took me a long time to understand  last week Sky News spoke to a Londoner  wanting to fight in Syria having been  influenced by the call to jihad in  videos online and what's innovative  about this project is it sits in the  place where young Muslims might seek  answers to questions about jihad do you  genuinely think that going out there to  fight and potentially be killed will  affect those suffering in a positive way  it offers an alternative to  radicalization but cuts a fine line you  get democracy or get hypocrisy you get  margin for error we get the label of  terror  he hopes to save the lives of young  Muslims who believe that jihad has  become some call of duty that is  mimicked for them through their  PlayStation experience that they now  think they can do for real in some  far-off land for a cause they don't  truly understand and furthermore it  hopefully starts to expel myths and  stereotypes and prejudices that  non-muslims may have about Islam because  the only Islam they hear about is one  that they tend to fear nowadays and the  Creator believes that some who have  followed the journey of Abdullah X have  as a result veered from quite a  different journey into radicalization  Jason Farrell Afghan youth
 

